      "why": "iterative has the smallest datapath (one shared shift/add stage, no per-stage registers), so it is the natural low-power candidate. But it emits one result every N+3 cycles, so throughput = Fmax/(N+3). To clear 50 MSPS it needs a high Fmax and a small N, which pushes n_iter toward the low end of the accuracy band. I keep data_width/n_iter in the band that can still reach max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (accuracy_bits >= 12) while keeping N small enough that Fmax/(N+3) >= 50. angle_guard/frac_guard kept tight to limit area/power; rounding explored to see if round buys accuracy bits cheaply."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "I am restricted to iterative and unrolled_k, both of which emit one result every several cycles (iterative: N+3, unrolled_k: ceil(N/k)+3). The throughput constraint (>= 50 MSPS) is the binding one: it forces a high Fmax and a small per-result cycle count, which in turn forces small n_iter and small data_width. The accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12, i.e. accuracy_bits >= 12) then sets the floor on n_iter/data_width. The feasible window is therefore narrow: n_iter roughly 12-18, data_width roughly 14-18, with angle_guard/frac_guard kept minimal to protect Fmax and power. I concentrate the entire budget on iterative first because it has the lowest per-cycle register/area overhead and thus the best power_index among the two families; unrolled_k only helps throughput via k but adds combinational depth (k chained rotations) that lowers Fmax and adds area, so it is unlikely to beat iterative on power once throughput is met. I will confirm feasibility and the throughput/accuracy boundary with iterative, and if it cannot reach 50 MSPS at accuracy_bits >= 12 I will fall back to unrolled_k in a later round. Ranges are kept tight to focus the 100-eval budget on the real trade-off (power vs accuracy_bits) rather than wasting evals on infeasible wide/long configurations."
}

